      "longDescription": "Festival Z is a performing arts festival that takes place annually over the course of one week in Girona.\n\nThe festival is committed to supporting companies and artists under 30 years of age, with the aim of reaching diverse audiences and fostering emerging creative voices, and we have been collaborating for the past five years on the creative direction of the festival.\n\nThe festival chooses a concept for each edition, accompanied by a claim that synthesizes the theme. This is the starting point of the creative process, whose objective is to match this concept with the general identity of the festival.\n\nWe've worked hand in hand during each edition of the festival to establish a unique and strong identity that represents the festival and its values; a playful, mischievous, disruptive, provocative and eye-catching event. The goal of this, besides obviously having a functional graphic identity, is to be able to visually connect it, year after year, to each edition's concept.\n\nFor the 2026 edition, and after a few years of carrying over adaptations of the original identity designed in the first edition, back in 2021, I decided to proactively propose a rebranding. The goal was to achieve an identity that represented the festival, as it had evolved a lot since the previous visual identity was designed, and to clearly differentiate the identity of the festival (same across editions) from the identity of each edition (different every year).\n\nThe outcome was an isologotype, born from the hand symbol in the original logo (representing how close the festival is to the public and artists), turned horizontal so it's also working as a megaphone (loud and provocative) and combined with a custom font with the name of the festival.\n\nWhen it comes to designing the identity of an edition, once the team has a clear concept, the process starts by translating it to a visual shape that looks and feels like the festival. This year's concept was \"A poc a poc i bon teatre\", a saying in Catalan that would translate to \"Slow and steady wins the race\". Through the years, the festival started to develop a bestiary of mascots that were being used to represent each edition's concept, and for the last edition, it was a long-legged turtle that transmitted this idea that doing things slowly often takes you to better results.\n\nOnce the visual concept is approved, tickets, flyers, banners, accreditations, festival program, social media assets, bus vinyls, posters and some other formats are designed and prepared for print. With this, every year the city of Girona dresses in Festival Z pink for a few days, reaching thousands of people with a new and memorable identity.",
